From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel <daniel@matv.io>
Date: Mon, 11 Aug 2026 00:00:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] ipa: soft: cap AGC exposure ceiling at the frame length

AGC-RAMP-EFFICIENCY FIX (upstream candidate; observed on fajita's
IMX376/IMX371, applies to any sensor on the soft ISP). The soft AGC
(src/ipa/simple/algorithms/agc.cpp) ramps exposure up to
configuration.agc.exposureMax and only then engages analogue gain.
exposureMax is taken verbatim from the V4L2 EXPOSURE control's max,
which the sensor driver reports for the MAXIMUM frame length, i.e. the
maximum VBLANK (on fajita's IMX376: 65515 lines = VTS_MAX - 10). But
the soft IPA never programs VBLANK -- it only ever writes EXPOSURE,
ANALOGUE_GAIN and FOCUS -- so the frame length is pinned at its
minimum (outputSize.height + vblank.min(); IMX376: 1080 + 2796 = 3876)
for the whole session, and the sensor cannot integrate longer than the
frame period. Every exposure code above ~minFrameLength is clamped by
the hardware to the same integration time, so the AGC spends ~30
frames ramping exposure 3876 -> 65515 (10%/frame) with no brightness
gain before it finally switches to analogue gain -- low-light scenes
brighten far too slowly.

Cap exposureMax at context_.sensorInfo.minFrameLength (the fixed frame
length the soft IPA actually runs at) so the ramp hands off to gain as
soon as real integration is maxed. The framework fills minFrameLength
from outputSize.height + vblank.min() (camera_sensor_legacy.cpp), and
it is already available via context_.sensorInfo (set in init()). The
change only ever LOWERS the exposure ceiling, so it cannot cause
over-exposure or oscillation: the AGC's up/down hysteresis is
untouched, exposure simply saturates sooner and analogue gain (bounded
by againMax) takes over. Guarded against unpopulated frame-length info
(minFrameLength == 0) and never raises the ceiling, so sensors that
already report a frame-length-limited exposure max are unaffected.

Pairs with the imx376/imx371 CameraSensorHelper (previous patch): with
the helper, analogue gain now seeds at 1.0x and ramps real multiples
1.0x->16.0x, and with this cap the exposure ramp reaches that hand-off
promptly -- together they fix the "far too dark" dusk/indoor rendering.
---
 src/ipa/simple/soft_simple.cpp | 13 +++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 13 insertions(+)

--- a/src/ipa/simple/soft_simple.cpp
+++ b/src/ipa/simple/soft_simple.cpp
@@ -229,5 +229,18 @@ int IPASoftSimple::configure(const IPAConfigInfo &configInfo)
 	context_.configuration.agc.exposureMin = exposureInfo.min().get<int32_t>();
 	context_.configuration.agc.exposureMax = exposureInfo.max().get<int32_t>();
+	/*
+	 * The soft IPA never programs VBLANK, so the frame length is fixed
+	 * at its minimum for the whole session and integration cannot exceed
+	 * it. The EXPOSURE control max is reported for the maximum frame
+	 * length, though, so cap the AGC's exposure ceiling at the actual
+	 * (minimum) frame length -- otherwise the ramp wastes many frames
+	 * driving exposure into values the sensor clamps to the frame period
+	 * before it hands off to analogue gain (too dark, too slow, in low
+	 * light). Guarded so it is a no-op when frame-length info is missing.
+	 */
+	int32_t frameLengthMax = static_cast<int32_t>(context_.sensorInfo.minFrameLength);
+	if (frameLengthMax > 0 && frameLengthMax < context_.configuration.agc.exposureMax)
+		context_.configuration.agc.exposureMax = frameLengthMax;
 	if (!context_.configuration.agc.exposureMin) {
 		LOG(IPASoft, Warning) << "Minimum exposure is zero, that can't be linear";
 		context_.configuration.agc.exposureMin = 1;
